  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "If ${F}^{\u2032}(t)=\u27e85{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9,$ then $F(t)=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \u27e8\\text{\u00a0}5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \u27e85{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9\\text{d}t & =\u27e8\\int 5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{t}^{5}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9+\u27e8{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration.",
